EZ2DIGITIZE - Third-party components and licenses
=================================================

EZ2DIGITIZE itself is licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later (see LICENSE).

This file lists every third-party component, grouped by how it reaches the
user. Update it in the same commit that adds, removes or re-versions a
component. Before each release, check it against what the installer
actually contains.

Rules (see CLAUDE.md):
  - Every bundled component must have a GPL-3.0-compatible license.
  - Never bundle components under non-commercial (NC) or other
    non-free licenses; users may install those themselves as plugins.
  - For every bundled GPL/LGPL/AGPL binary, the exact corresponding source
    is published alongside the release.


1. Bundled Python dependencies (shipped in the installer)
----------------------------------------------------------

PySide6 / Shiboken6 (Qt for Python)
  License:  LGPL-3.0-only (also offered under GPL-2.0/GPL-3.0 and commercial terms)
  Source:   https://code.qt.io/cgit/pyside/pyside-setup.git/
  Notes:    Bundles Qt 6 libraries, which are LGPL-3.0 except for some add-on
            modules that are GPL-3.0 only. Both are compatible with this
            project's license. The 3D viewer uses Qt WebEngine (LGPL-3.0),
            which contains Chromium and its third-party components (mostly
            BSD-style, MIT, Apache-2.0, LGPL; listed in Qt's documentation,
            "Qt WebEngine Licensing", and chrome://credits).

Pillow
  License:  MIT-CMU (HPND)
  Source:   https://github.com/python-pillow/Pillow
  Notes:    Photo checks (size, EXIF, sharpness; ez2digitize.core.photos).
            Its wheels bundle (Linux, 12.x): libjpeg-turbo (IJG/BSD-3-Clause),
            libpng (libpng), libtiff (libtiff), libwebp and libsharpyuv
            (BSD-3-Clause), libavif (BSD-2-Clause), OpenJPEG (BSD-2-Clause),
            FreeType (FTL), HarfBuzz (MIT), LittleCMS (MIT), Brotli (MIT),
            zstd (BSD-3-Clause), liblzma (0BSD), libxcb and libXau (MIT).
            Their license texts are in the wheel's dist-info/licenses.

pillow-heif
  License:  BSD-3-Clause (its code); the wheel bundles libheif and libde265
            (LGPL-3.0) and x265 (GPL-2.0-or-later), so the binary wheel as a
            whole is GPL; all compatible with GPL-3.0-or-later.
  Source:   https://github.com/bigcat88/pillow_heif
  Notes:    Decodes iPhone HEIC photos into JPEG copies on import
            (ez2digitize.core.heic). License texts in its dist-info.

segno
  License:  BSD-3-Clause
  Source:   https://github.com/heuer/segno
  Notes:    QR codes for phone upload (pure Python, no dependencies).

pupil-apriltags (with the AprilTag 3 library)
  License:  MIT (the Python binding); BSD-2-Clause (AprilTag, (C) 2013-2015
            The Regents of The University of Michigan)
  Source:   https://github.com/pupil-labs/apriltags
            (AprilTag: https://github.com/AprilRobotics/apriltag)
  Notes:    Finds the printed scale markers (tag36h11) in the photos and
            draws them for the printable sheet (ez2digitize.markers). Chosen
            over OpenCV's ArUco detector: OpenCV's Linux wheels bundle
            OpenSSL 1.1.1, whose license isn't GPL-compatible. License text
            in its dist-info.

ONNX Runtime
  License:  MIT
  Source:   https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime
  Notes:    Runs the masking model, in a separate worker process
            (ez2digitize.mask_worker). The licenses of the components it
            contains are in its ThirdPartyNotices.txt (in the package). Its
            telemetry exists only in Windows builds; it collects nothing on
            Linux or macOS. Depends on flatbuffers (Apache-2.0), protobuf
            (BSD-3-Clause) and packaging (Apache-2.0 or BSD-2-Clause).

NumPy
  License:  BSD-3-Clause
  Source:   https://github.com/numpy/numpy
  Notes:    Arrays for the masking worker. Its Linux wheels bundle OpenBLAS
            (BSD-3-Clause) and libgfortran/libquadmath (GPL-3.0 with the GCC
            Runtime Library Exception); license texts in its dist-info.

three.js 0.186.1 (JavaScript, vendored in ez2digitize/ui/viewer_web/vendor)
  License:  MIT
  Source:   https://github.com/mrdoob/three.js (npm package "three")
  Notes:    The 3D viewer's renderer, with its OrbitControls, PLYLoader,
            GLTFLoader, BufferGeometryUtils, SkeletonUtils and Pass add-ons.
            License text in vendor/three/LICENSE; pinned by npm integrity hash
            (tools/viewer/fetch_vendor.py, vendor/VENDOR.json).

Spark 2.3.1 (JavaScript, vendored in ez2digitize/ui/viewer_web/vendor)
  License:  MIT
  Source:   https://github.com/sparkjsdev/spark (npm "@sparkjsdev/spark")
  Notes:    Gaussian splat rendering in the viewer. Its bundle includes
            fflate (MIT) and a WebAssembly module built from Spark's own
            code. License text in vendor/spark/LICENSE.

The masking model is not bundled: it is downloaded on first use into the
user's cache and checked against a pinned sha256 (ez2digitize.masks):

isnet-general-use (ISNet, "Highly Accurate Dichotomous Image Segmentation",
Qin et al.)
  License:  Apache-2.0 (code and weights)
  Source:   https://github.com/xuebinqin/DIS
  Notes:    The ONNX export published by the rembg project (MIT),
            https://github.com/danielgatis/rembg/releases/tag/v0.0.0

COLMAP's vocabulary tree is not bundled either: it is downloaded when a
project has more than 200 photos, into the same cache, checked against the
sha256 COLMAP 4.2.1 itself pins (ez2digitize.backends.colmap.VOCAB_TREE):

vocab_tree_faiss_flickr100K_words256K.bin
  License:  none stated for the file itself; published by the COLMAP project
            (BSD-3-Clause) as an asset of its 3.11.1 release, and the
            file COLMAP itself downloads. It holds visual words trained on
            the Flickr100k images, not the images.
  Source:   https://github.com/colmap/colmap/releases/tag/3.11.1


2. External backends (invoked as separate processes)
----------------------------------------------------

The AppImage and the macOS app bundle COLMAP and OpenMVS, built by
tools/backends/build.sh with all their dependencies linked statically, and
the Brush release binary. Their license texts are inside the app, in
backends/licenses/ (the copyright file of every library linked in), and the
app shows this file under Help -> Licenses.

COLMAP 4.2.1    BSD-3-Clause        https://github.com/colmap/colmap
                with code it compiles in: LSD (AGPL-3.0-or-later),
                PoissonRecon (MIT) and VLFeat (BSD-2-Clause) from its own
                tree, and PoseLib (BSD-3-Clause) and faiss 1.14.1 (MIT),
                which its build fetches; their license texts are in
                backends/licenses as colmap-<name>.txt
OpenMVS v2.4.0  AGPL-3.0            https://github.com/cdcseacave/openMVS
                (depends on CGAL, parts of which are GPL-3.0+), with the
                upstream fix in tools/backends/patches applied
Brush 0.3.0     Apache-2.0          https://github.com/ArthurBrussee/brush
                The release binary, unmodified, with its LICENSE under
                backends/licenses/brush, and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt there:
                the license, copyright and NOTICE texts of the 545 Rust
                crates compiled into it (MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC, Zlib,
                MPL-2.0, Unicode-3.0 and the like), collected from Brush's
                Cargo.lock by tools/packaging/brush_notices.py.
GCC runtime     GPL-3.0 with the GCC Runtime Library Exception
                (libgomp, libgfortran, libquadmath; AppImage only, copied
                from the build machine for the backends; in the Windows
                backend builds, the MinGW runtime DLLs LAPACK needs, if any)
libomp          Apache-2.0 with LLVM exceptions (macOS app only, Homebrew's)
MSVC runtime    Microsoft's redistributable runtime (Windows backend builds
                only: vcomp140.dll, the OpenMP runtime, and any C++ runtime
                DLL it needs; the rest is linked statically). Part of the
                compiler, so a System Library in GPL-3.0 section 1 terms;
                redistributed under the Visual Studio license's terms for
                its redistributable files.
FFmpeg          LGPL-2.1+ or GPL-2.0+ depending on build configuration
                                    https://ffmpeg.org/
                Used for video import (decoding, JPEG frames). Not bundled
                yet: the user's installed FFmpeg is run as a process.


Source for the bundled backends. COLMAP and OpenMVS in the apps are built
from the tags, the vcpkg release and the patches pinned in
tools/backends/build.sh, which records them in backends/BUILDINFO.json; all
of it is in this repository and the upstream projects. Development builds
(CI artifacts) point there. tools/backends/collect_sources.sh packs the
exact corresponding source (both projects, the patches, vcpkg and every
port's source archive; the Backend sources workflow builds it), and each
release publishes that archive next to the binaries. Anyone who got a binary
can also ask for it through the project's issue tracker.


